│ patch_name               │ trie_safe_reorder                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The original implementation could corrupt row data and return invalid metadata. It        │
│                          │ repeatedly rebuilt DataFrames from `.values`, used asynchronous completion order as row   │
│                          │ positions, appended and removed an `original_index` helper column, merged reordered data  │
│                          │ back by value, and finally returned an empty `column_orderings` list. Those behaviors can │
│                          │ detach values from their source rows and violate the required per-row ordering contract.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The stdout shows the algorithm reached its recursive preprocessing path, but no           │
│                          │ validation traceback was emitted. The likely validation failure is therefore structural:  │
│                          │ data/row identity was not reliably preserved and the required column-ordering result was  │
│                          │ empty. The replacement below keeps every source value, uses stable row identities,        │
│                          │ creates valid per-row column orderings, supports merged-column adjacency and one-way      │
│                          │ dependency ordering, and selects among bounded column-order candidates using the exact    │
│                          │ sorted serialized-row LCP objective.                                                      │
