│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_safe_reorder                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The original implementation failed validation because it could lose row identity and      │
│                          │ corrupt the relationship between reordered values and columns. In particular, it created  │
│                          │ temporary `original_index` columns, rebuilt frames from `.values.tolist()`, merged/sorted │
│                          │ rows afterward, and finally returned an empty `column_orderings` list. The threaded       │
│                          │ recursion also wrote rows using completion order rather than source-row order, so values  │
│                          │ could be assigned to the wrong rows.                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The replacement keeps every original row and cell intact, uses object dtype for mixed     │
│                          │ data, returns a valid column ordering for every output row, and does not add helper       │
│                          │ columns or merge/drop data. It evaluates a bounded set of column-order candidates using   │
│                          │ the actual serialized character-prefix Trie objective (sorted-string LCP sum), then       │
│                          │ returns the best candidate. It also supports a bounded conditional grouping candidate     │
│                          │ while preserving supplied merge/dependency ordering constraints.                          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
